cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Plumbline: a lossless JPEG decoder that is right, or says so"
message: >-
  If you use this software or its conformance corpus in work you publish,
  please cite it. If you use the corpus to evaluate a different decoder,
  citing it is what makes your result checkable by someone else.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "T."
    given-names: "Rungroj"
repository-code: "https://github.com/yahkuza99/plumbline"
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.1.0
abstract: >-
  A decoder for ITU-T T.81 Annex H lossless JPEG (DICOM transfer syntaxes
  1.2.840.10008.1.2.4.57 and .70) built on a single rule: decode correctly or
  raise, never return plausible wrong pixels. It ships three independent
  implementations behind one entry point — compiled C, numba, and a pure-Python
  reference written to be read against the specification clause by clause — and
  a conformance corpus that enumerates the format's entire legal parameter
  space rather than sampling it, together with a battery of malformed frames a
  decoder must refuse.

  The corpus is built by encoding chosen images, so each case asserts a round
  trip rather than one implementation's reading of the standard. That
  distinction is not academic: two misreadings of Annex H were shared by this
  project's encoder and all three of its decoders, agreed with each other
  perfectly, and were caught only by comparison against an implementation that
  shares no code with them.
keywords:
  - lossless JPEG
  - ITU-T T.81
  - DICOM
  - medical imaging
  - image codec
  - conformance testing
  - silent data corruption
